EXT. MARATHON AID STATION - MILE 22 - 3 A.M.

A folding table under a sodium streetlight. Paper cups in rows. A cooler of orange slices. The course winds into the dark.

OTTILIE MARSH, 60s, in a reflective vest, tops up cups from a jug. PAVEL DVORAK, 30s, a volunteer, sits on a camp chair and stares at the road. YOSHIRO TANABE, 40s, a medic, arranges bandages in a tackle box.

Ottilie finishes the row. She wipes her hands. She glances at the road, then at Pavel. He does not look at her.

She slides a cup across the table. He takes it. He holds it without drinking.

YOSHIRO
Anyone coming?

OTTILIE
Not yet.

PAVEL
They'll be a while. The leaders passed an hour ago.

YOSHIRO
I'd run it again.

OTTILIE
You would not.

YOSHIRO
I would.

She smiles. He does not. Pavel gets up and stands at the edge of the light. He puts the cup down on the table without a word. After a moment she follows his gaze.

OTTILIE
It was last year, wasn't it.

PAVEL
I'm fine.

She walks to the table, takes up the jug, and carries it to him. She holds out a full cup, and he accepts it with both hands.

YOSHIRO
Headlights.

They all turn. A lone runner appears at the edge of the dark, slow and swaying. Pavel steps forward with the cup held out in front of him like an offering.
